# ── Render deployment environment variables ─────────────────────────────────
# Don't commit this file with real secrets filled in. On Render, add these
# one by one under: your service → Environment → Environment Variables.
# This file is just a convenient reference of what to add.

# --- Required ---

NODE_ENV=production

# Do NOT set PORT yourself -- Render injects it automatically and the app
# already reads process.env.PORT. Setting it manually can break routing.

# Serves the built React app from the same service as the API (single
# Render web service, not two). Must match where `npm run build` outputs
# the client, relative to the repo root.
STATIC_DIR=client/dist

# SQLite file path. Render's filesystem is EPHEMERAL by default -- anything
# written here is wiped on every deploy and on most restarts. To actually
# keep bookings between deploys:
#   1. Render dashboard -> your service -> Disks -> Add Disk
#   2. Mount path: /var/data   (size: 1 GB is plenty for this app)
#   3. Set DATABASE_PATH to a file inside that mount, as below.
# If you skip this, the app still works, but the database resets on every deploy.
DATABASE_PATH=/var/data/appointments.db

# Signs customer-access, contact, and admin tokens. Generate a real random
# value -- do not reuse this example one. Run locally:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
AUTH_SECRET=97c98111521bcc9a4d642fd4639f431807fe1de6ab8c056f8cfdc62372940cd0

# Bootstraps the FIRST staff account only, on a brand-new database. If you're
# updating an already-deployed app that had these set before per-staff
# accounts existed, leave them as-is -- your existing login keeps working
# with zero changes. To add more staff accounts later (or reset a password),
# run this from your own machine against the SAME database Render uses --
# see "Managing staff accounts on Render" below.
# Generate a hash for your own password locally (never put the plaintext
# password in an env var) by running, from the repo root, after `npm install`:
#   npm run hash-password -w server -- "YourStrongPasswordHere"
# then paste the printed value (starts with "scrypt$...") below.
ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=scrypt$REPLACE_ME_RUN_THE_COMMAND_ABOVE

# --- Optional (defaults shown; only add if you want to change them) ---

# LOG_LEVEL=info
# BOOKING_HORIZON_DAYS=30
# BOOKING_MIN_LEAD_MINUTES=30
# DATA_RETENTION_DAYS=90
# RETENTION_CHECK_INTERVAL_HOURS=24
# REMINDER_CHECK_INTERVAL_MINUTES=60
# PRIVACY_CONTACT_EMAIL=privacy@yourdomain.example
# ADMIN_MAX_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS=5
# ADMIN_LOCKOUT_MINUTES=15

# Only needed if the frontend is hosted separately from this API (it isn't,
# in the default single-service setup -- leave unset).
# CORS_ORIGIN=https://your-frontend.example

# --- Managing staff accounts on Render ---
#
# Staff accounts live in the SQLite database on your persistent disk, not in
# an env var, so adding a staff member or resetting a password needs to run
# against that live database. Two ways to do it:
#
#   1. If your Render plan has a Shell tab: your service -> Shell, then run
#        npm run create-staff-user -w server -- <username> <password>
#
#   2. On any plan (no shell needed): sign in to /admin/login with an
#      existing account, then call the authenticated API from your own
#      machine using that account's token:
#        curl -X POST https://your-app.onrender.com/api/auth/login \
#          -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
#          -d '{"username":"admin","password":"YourCurrentPassword"}'
#        # copy the "token" from the response, then:
#        curl -X POST https://your-app.onrender.com/api/admin/staff-users \
#          -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
#          -H 'Authorization: Bearer <token from above>' \
#          -d '{"username":"sarah","password":"sarahs-new-password-123"}'
#      This also works to reset a password for an existing (even locked-out)
#      username -- use a *different* account's token to do the reset if the
#      one you're resetting is the one that's locked.

#      This also works to reset a password for an existing (even locked-out)
#      username -- use a *different* account's token to do the reset if the
#      one you're resetting is the one that's locked.
#
#   3. Locked out of every account, no shell, and no other working login?
#      Set BOTH of these on Render (Environment tab), then redeploy:
#        EMERGENCY_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
#        EMERGENCY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=YourNewPasswordHere12+chars
#      On the next boot, that account's password is forcibly reset (and any
#      lockout cleared) automatically -- no shell or curl needed. The server
#      log will show a warning confirming it happened.
#      CRITICAL: remove both variables again right after and redeploy once
#      more. If you leave them set, EVERY future restart repeats the reset,
#      silently undoing any password change you make through the app later.
